<p>People search these one at a time, usually while looking at the dog: why is my dog crying, why are my dog's eyes red, why does my dog have dandruff. They feel like separate problems, and mostly they are. But the first two overlap more than you'd think, because "crying" in a dog can mean a noise or wet eyes, and wet eyes and red eyes often end up being the same vet visit. Dandruff is the odd one out and the least urgent, so it gets a shorter section at the end.</p>

<h2>"Crying" Usually Means One of Two Things</h2>

<p>Whining and whimpering are a vocal signal: the question is what your dog is trying to tell you. Wet, running, or stained eyes are a physical symptom: the question is what's irritating or blocking the eye. The causes barely overlap, so be clear about which one you're seeing first.</p>

<h3>The sound: whining is communication, and context is the decoder</h3>

<p>The AKC points out that puppies learn to whine to get their mother's attention, and many dogs keep the habit into adulthood, asking for food, water, a trip outside, or just a hand on the head. The sound itself doesn't tell you much. The situation does:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>At the front door when you come home:</strong> usually excitement. Wiggling body, loose tail, nothing to fix.</li>
<li><strong>Standing at the back door or next to an empty bowl:</strong> a request. If it stops the moment the need is met, that's your answer.</li>
<li><strong>Only when you leave, or starting as soon as you're out of sight:</strong> this points toward what VCA calls separation-related distress, where vocalizing comes alongside pacing, damage around doors, drooling, or house-soiling, almost only when the dog is alone.</li>
<li><strong>On the exam table, in the car, during a thunderstorm:</strong> fear or stress, tied to a specific trigger.</li>
<li><strong>When getting up, climbing stairs, being picked up, or touched in one spot:</strong> this is the one to take seriously. Whining that lines up with movement or handling is a pain signal until proven otherwise.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last one is the version people talk themselves out of. A quiet dog who suddenly whines when lying down, or yelps when lifted, isn't being dramatic. VCA adds a point that's easy to miss: when a dog who used to be fine alone suddenly isn't, pain or a condition that makes them need to go out more urgently can be behind it, and in seniors, cognitive decline can show up as new anxiety. New whining with no obvious reason is a reason for a vet exam, not just a training project.</p>

<p>If the pattern looks more like anxiety than pain, our <a href="/tools/dog-anxiety-guide/">dog anxiety guide</a> walks through the common triggers and what tends to help.</p>

<h3>The tears: do dogs actually cry emotionally?</h3>

<p>The long-standing answer is no. The AKC says there's no scientific evidence that dogs, or any other animals, produce tears in response to what they're feeling. Dogs have feelings and they have tear ducts, but the link between the two that humans have hasn't been shown. The AKC also notes that in dogs, tear fluid drains back toward the nose and throat rather than spilling down the face, which is part of why visible tears usually mean something is off.</p>

<p>There is one genuinely interesting wrinkle. In 2022, researchers in Japan (Murata, Kikusui, and colleagues) published a study in <em>Current Biology</em> that measured dogs' tear volume with the Schirmer tear test, the paper-strip test vets use to check for dry eye. Tear volume rose significantly when dogs were reunited with their owners, but not with a familiar non-owner. Applying oxytocin, the hormone tied to social bonding, to dogs' eyes also raised tear volume, suggesting it may drive the effect. And people rated photos of dogs with artificial tears more positively than photos without.</p>

<p>Two caveats. The study measured tear volume on a test strip; it didn't show tears running down faces at the front door. And it's one study, which the authors frame as suggesting emotion-linked tears may help dogs and people bond, not as proof that dogs weep. A fun thing to know, not a reason to assume a wet-faced dog is happy.</p>

<h3>When the eyes really are watering</h3>

<p>Constant wet eyes have a name, epiphora, which VCA describes as an overflow of tears: either the eye makes too many, or they can't drain. Common causes from the AKC and VCA:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Allergies and irritants:</strong> pollen, dust, smoke, and dander can all make eyes water.</li>
<li><strong>A speck of dirt:</strong> tearing should be temporary. If it isn't, check with your vet.</li>
<li><strong>A blocked tear duct:</strong> eyes stay damp and can get irritated.</li>
<li><strong>Face shape:</strong> in flat-faced breeds, tears often can't reach the duct and roll off the face, and hair can block the opening.</li>
<li><strong>Entropion:</strong> an eyelid that rolls inward so the lashes and hair rub the eye. VCA notes it's usually diagnosed in puppies under a year old, in breeds including the Shar Pei, Chow Chow, Labrador and Golden Retriever, and English Bulldog. Squinting plus heavy tearing in a young dog is worth a vet visit; the fix is surgery, and waiting can scar the cornea.</li>
<li><strong>A scratched cornea or an infection:</strong> the AKC flags yellow or bloody discharge as a sign of infection, and pawing at the eye or blinking a lot as signs of a scratch.</li>
</ul>

<p>The reddish-brown stains under many white dogs' eyes come from this. VCA says no remover has proven fully effective, some products may harm the eyes, and anything with hydrogen peroxide should stay well away from them. Keeping the area clean, dry, and trimmed is the low-risk option.</p>

<p>Notice how many of those causes also make the eye red. That's the bridge to the next question.</p>

<h2>Red Eyes Run From "Dust" to "Today"</h2>

<p>Redness shows up with mild irritation and with conditions that can cost a dog their sight. The goal isn't to name the cause yourself, but to sort which group you're in.</p>

<h3>The common, usually manageable causes</h3>

<p><strong>Conjunctivitis</strong> is inflammation of the pink tissue lining the eyelids and covering the white of the eye. VCA lists the signs as cloudy, yellow, or greenish discharge, squinting, and redness or swelling, with causes ranging from infections and seasonal allergies to smoke, foreign bodies, and eyelid problems. Most cases do very well, but it's often secondary to something else, so the vet's job is finding what's underneath. VCA also warns against reusing leftover eye medication from a past episode, which may not help and may harm.</p>

<p><strong>Dry eye</strong> (keratoconjunctivitis sicca, or KCS) is easy to mix up with plain conjunctivitis. Too few tears leave the eye red, painful, and dull, often with thick yellowish discharge. VCA notes it mostly affects middle-aged to older dogs, especially breeds like the American Cocker Spaniel, Cavalier King Charles Spaniel, Shih Tzu, Pug, and Westie. It's diagnosed with that same Schirmer tear test and usually managed with ongoing treatment.</p>

<p><strong>A trapped eyelash, grass seed, or bit of debris</strong> can turn one eye red fast. If you can see something lodged there, don't dig at it; let the vet look, since pawing and rubbing can scratch the cornea.</p>

<h3>The ones that can't wait</h3>

<p><strong>Glaucoma</strong> is raised pressure inside the eye. VCA's signs: eye pain (half-closing or rubbing the eye, flinching when that side of the head is touched), watery discharge, lethargy, a bulging eye, a red, engorged white of the eye, and a cloudy or bluish cornea. Blindness can come quickly unless the pressure drops, and VCA states plainly that acute glaucoma is an emergency. Higher-risk breeds include Cocker Spaniels, Basset Hounds, Beagles, Shih Tzus, and Siberian Huskies.</p>

<p><strong>Uveitis</strong> is inflammation inside the eye. VCA describes severe pain, intense redness, a squinted-shut eye, light avoidance, and cloudiness. Causes include infections like Lyme disease, high blood pressure, diabetes, immune disease, and trauma, so a red, painful eye can sometimes be the visible sign of a whole-body illness. It looks a lot like glaucoma; vets tell them apart by eye pressure, high with glaucoma and low with uveitis.</p>

<h3>Sorting it in practice</h3>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>What you're seeing</th><th>Reasonable next step</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Mild pinkness in both eyes, clear watery tears, dog is comfortable, recent dust, smoke, or pollen</td><td>Watch for a day or two; call your vet if it doesn't settle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Redness with yellow or green discharge, mild squinting, no cloudiness</td><td>Book a vet visit in the next day or so</td></tr>
<tr><td>Something visible in the eye that won't flush out, or a red eye after a scratch or a run through brush</td><td>Same-day vet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sudden redness with obvious pain, eye held shut, cloudy or bluish surface, bulging, or the dog bumping into things</td><td>Same-day or emergency vet, without waiting to see</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The pattern to remember is short: <strong>sudden, painful, cloudy, or affecting vision means today.</strong> Mild, comfortable, and clear can usually wait a little. When you're not sure which side you're on, our <a href="/tools/pet-first-aid-guide/">pet first aid guide</a> can help you decide whether to call ahead or just go.</p>

<h2>And the One Nobody Expects: Dandruff</h2>

<p>Dandruff is the least urgent of the three, and often it's just dry skin. PetMD lists causes including allergies, low humidity, a lack of omega fatty acids, hormone imbalances, and skin infections, with some breeds, like Cocker Spaniels and Westies, genetically prone.</p>

<p>Winter is the classic season: heated indoor air dries out skin. Too many baths can strip natural oils, so dry-skinned dogs often do better with fewer baths and a gentle shampoo; our guide to <a href="/blog/how-often-should-you-bathe-a-dog/">how often to bathe a dog</a> breaks that down by coat type. Brushing helps spread those oils. And PetMD is firm: never use human anti-dandruff shampoo on a dog.</p>

<p>Then there's the strange one: <strong>"walking dandruff."</strong> If the flakes seem to move, you may be seeing Cheyletiella mites, large and pale enough to look like crawling dandruff. VCA describes it as uncommon (modern flea control has made it rare) but highly contagious to dogs, cats, rabbits, and people, usually picked up at shelters, groomers, or anywhere dogs mix. The main sign is heavy, large-flake scaling along the back. Every pet in the house needs treatment at once, plus bedding, or it returns. People's infections clear once the pets are treated, but if someone at home suddenly has itchy skin, mention it to the vet.</p>

<p>Dandruff moves from "probably winter" to "worth an appointment" when it comes with constant scratching, hair loss, redness, odor, or changes in weight or energy, since PetMD lists hypothyroidism, diabetes, and other hormone conditions among the possible underlying causes.</p>

<h2>What Ties These Together</h2>

<p>Each sign has a harmless version and a serious one, and what separates them is rarely the sign itself. Whining at the door is happiness; whining when you touch a hip is pain. A pink eye after a dusty walk is irritation; a red, cloudy, squinted eye is a same-day problem. Flakes in January are dry air; flakes that move are mites. Notice when it started, what it comes with, and whether your dog seems comfortable, and you'll usually know which version you're looking at.</p>

